# Worked solutions: 4.38 - 4.46 (root locus) and 5.1 - 5.31 (frequency response, part 1)
S = {
"4.38": ("A", "Purpose of the root locus.", "The root locus shows the open-loop poles and zeros (1), how the closed-loop poles, and hence the transient response, change with the gain $K$ (2), and the range of $K$ for stability, from the imaginary-axis crossing (3). All three: **(a)**."),
"4.39": ("A", "Asymptote angles $\\dfrac{(2k+1)180^\\circ}{P-Z}$.", "Poles: $-2\\pm j2,\\ -1,\\ 0$ ($P=4$); zero: $-3$ ($Z=1$). $P-Z=3$.\n$\\theta=\\dfrac{180^\\circ(2k+1)}{3}=60^\\circ,\\ 180^\\circ,\\ 300^\\circ\\ (=-60^\\circ)$: **(a)**."),
"4.40": ("A", "Angle criterion on the real axis.", "(I) True: the angle contributions of a conjugate pair cancel, so they do not alter the real-axis rule.\n(II) True: for a point on the real axis the two angles add to $2\\pi$ ($\\equiv0$), which leaves the odd-multiple-of-$\\pi$ condition unchanged. That is the reason for (I).\n\n**(a)**."),
"4.41": (None, "Effect of adding a pole to the loop transfer function.", "1. The locus is pulled towards the right half-plane: true.\n3. The response becomes slower: true.\n2. Whether the steady-state error grows depends on how the pole is written. A pole in the form $\\dfrac1{1+sT}$ leaves the error constants unchanged, while $\\dfrac1{s+p}$ reduces them. The statement is therefore ambiguous, and no answer key has been set."),
"4.42": ("B", "Breakaway: $dK/ds=0$.", "$K=-s(s+1)(s+2)=-(s^3+3s^2+2s)$.\n$\\dfrac{dK}{ds}=-(3s^2+6s+2)=0\\Rightarrow s=-1\\pm0.577=-0.423$ or $-1.577$.\nOnly $-0.42$ lies on the real-axis locus (between $0$ and $-1$): **(b)**."),
"4.43": ("C", "Asymptote angles for $P-Z=3$.", "$\\theta=\\dfrac{(2k+1)180^\\circ}{3}=60^\\circ,\\ 180^\\circ,\\ 300^\\circ$: **(c)**."),
"4.44": ("D", "Departure and arrival angles on the real axis.", "On the real axis the locus leaves a real pole, or enters a real zero, along the axis, at $0^\\circ$ or $180^\\circ$: **(d)**."),
"4.45": ("C", "Root locus technique.", "The root locus shows how the closed-loop poles, and thus transient response and stability, vary with loop gain: **(c)**."),
"4.46": ("D", "Breakaway: $dK/ds=0$.", "$K=-\\dfrac{(s+1)^2}{s+2}$.\n$\\dfrac{dK}{ds}=0\\Rightarrow2(s+1)(s+2)-(s+1)^2=(s+1)(s+3)=0\\Rightarrow s=-1,\\ -3$: **(d)**."),
"5.1": ("D", "Who contributed what.", "Bode: asymptotic (magnitude) plots (1). Evans: root-locus technique (3). Nyquist: polar plots and the stability criterion (2).\n\nA–1, B–3, C–2: **(d)**."),
"5.2": ("A", "Read PM and GM from the polar plot.", "At the gain crossover (unit circle) the plot makes $-30^\\circ$ with the positive real axis, i.e. $\\angle G=-30^\\circ$, so $PM=180^\\circ-30^\\circ=150^\\circ$.\nThe plot crosses the negative real axis at $-0.25$, so $GM=\\dfrac1{0.25}=4$.\n\n**(a)**."),
"5.3": ("B", "Phase crossover: $\\angle G(j\\omega)=-180^\\circ$.", "$\\phi=-\\left(\\tan^{-1}\\omega+\\tan^{-1}2\\omega+\\tan^{-1}3\\omega\\right)$.\nAt $\\omega=1$: $45^\\circ+63.4^\\circ+71.6^\\circ=180^\\circ$.\n$\\omega_{pc}=1$ rad/s: **(b)**."),
"5.4": ("B", "System type from the low-frequency behaviour of the plot.", "The plot comes in from infinity along a vertical asymptote, which means the phase at $\\omega\\to0$ is $-90^\\circ$. That is a type-1 system: **(b)**."),
"5.5": ("A", "Phase crossover and gain margin.", "$\\phi=-90^\\circ-\\tan^{-1}\\omega T_1-\\tan^{-1}\\omega T_2=-180^\\circ\\Rightarrow\\omega^2T_1T_2=1\\Rightarrow\\omega_{pc}=\\dfrac1{\\sqrt{T_1T_2}}$.\n$|G|=\\dfrac{1}{\\omega\\sqrt{(1+\\omega^2T_1^2)(1+\\omega^2T_2^2)}}=\\dfrac{T_1T_2}{T_1+T_2}$ there.\n$GM=\\dfrac1{|G|}=\\dfrac{T_1+T_2}{T_1T_2}$: **(a)**."),
"5.6": ("D", "High-frequency slope $=-20(P-Z)$ dB/decade.", "Poles: 2 at the origin + 4 complex $=6$. Zeros: 2.\nSlope $=-20(6-2)=-80$ dB/decade: **(d)**."),
"5.7": ("D", "A right-half-plane zero.", "A transfer function with a zero (or pole) in the right half of the $s$-plane is non-minimum phase: **(d)**."),
"5.8": ("A", "Phase of $\\dfrac{10}{s^2(1+0.5s)(1+s)}$ at the extremes.", "$\\phi(\\omega)=-180^\\circ-\\tan^{-1}0.5\\omega-\\tan^{-1}\\omega$.\n- $\\omega\\to0$: $\\phi\\to-180^\\circ$ from below (equivalent to $180^\\circ-\\epsilon$), magnitude $\\to\\infty$: second quadrant.\n- $\\omega\\to\\infty$: $\\phi\\to-360^\\circ$ from above (equivalent to a small positive angle), magnitude $\\to0$: first quadrant.\n\nStarts ($\\omega=\\infty$) in the first quadrant and terminates ($\\omega=0$) in the second: **(a)**."),
"5.9": ("C", "Type-1 system: the plot starts at $-j\\infty$.", "$G=\\dfrac{5}{s(1+0.1s)(1+0.01s)}$ has an integrator. As $\\omega\\to0$, $|G|\\to\\infty$ with phase $-90^\\circ$, and it ends at the origin with phase $-270^\\circ$.\nOnly plot (c) comes in from $-j\\infty$ and shows this wrap-around. (a), (b), (d) all start at finite points: **(c)**."),
"5.10": ("B", "Gain and phase margins.", "1. Both measure how close the polar plot comes to $-1+j0$: true.\n2. A stable non-minimum-phase system need not have positive margins: false.\n3. A stable minimum-phase system must have both margins positive: true.\n\nOnly 1 and 3: **(b)**."),
"5.11": ("B", "Non-minimum phase and Bode's gain-phase relation.", "(A) $G$ has a zero at $s=+25$ in the right half-plane: non-minimum phase, true.\n(R) For minimum-phase systems the magnitude plot determines the phase uniquely: true, but it does not explain why a zero at $+25$ makes (A) true.\n\n**(b)**."),
"5.12": ("B", "Bandwidth.", "(A) A smaller bandwidth filters out more high-frequency noise: true.\n(R) Bandwidth also sets how well the system follows (reproduces) the input: true.\nBoth are properties of bandwidth, but one is not the explanation of the other: **(b)**."),
"5.13": ("A", "Effect of an extra pole.", "(A) An extra pole slows the closed loop, so the rise time increases: true.\n(R) It reduces the bandwidth, and $t_r\\propto1/BW$, so this is exactly why the rise time rises: true.\n\n**(a)**."),
"5.14": ("C", "Effects of a phase-lag compensator.", "A lag compensator lowers the gain at higher frequency, which reduces the gain crossover frequency (2), the bandwidth (3) and the resonance peak (4). It does not reduce the velocity constant, in fact it allows a larger one for the same stability margin, so (1) is not an effect.\n\n2, 3 and 4: **(c)**."),
"5.15": ("D", "Read the margins from the table.", "Gain crossover ($|G|=1$) is at $\\omega=8$, where $\\angle G=-170^\\circ$. $PM=180^\\circ-170^\\circ=10^\\circ$.\nPhase crossover ($\\angle G=-180^\\circ$) is at $\\omega=10$, where $|G|=0.64$. $GM=20\\log\\dfrac1{0.64}=3.86$ dB.\n\n**(d)**."),
"5.16": ("B", "Match DC value and end phase of the Nyquist plot.", "The plot starts at $+1$ ($\\omega=0$), so the DC gain is 1. It ends at the origin tangent to the negative real axis, so the phase tends to $-180^\\circ$: two poles.\nThat is $\\dfrac1{(s+1)^2}$ (a third-order system would end at $-270^\\circ$, a first-order one at $-90^\\circ$, and $\\dfrac1{s^2+2s+2}$ has DC gain 0.5): **(b)**."),
"5.17": ("B", "Phase margin vs damping ratio.", "For the standard second-order loop $G=\\dfrac{\\omega_n^2}{s(s+2\\xi\\omega_n)}$,\n$PM=\\tan^{-1}\\dfrac{2\\xi}{\\sqrt{-2\\xi^2+\\sqrt{1+4\\xi^4}}}$: **(b)**."),
"5.18": ("B", "Bode magnitude plot: slopes and corner frequencies.", "1. The initial slope is $-20$ dB/decade: type 1. True.\n2. The extension of the $-20$ dB/decade line meets 0 dB at $\\omega=K_v$, which is $\\omega_3$: true.\n3. At $\\omega_1$ the level is $20\\log(\\omega_3/\\omega_1)$, and the $-40$ segment reaches 0 dB at $\\omega_2$, so $40\\log(\\omega_2/\\omega_1)=20\\log(\\omega_3/\\omega_1)$, i.e. $\\omega_2=\\sqrt{\\omega_1\\omega_3}$ (geometric, not arithmetic mean): false.\n\nOnly 1 and 2: **(b)**."),
"5.19": ("C", "High-frequency slope $=-20(P-Z)$ dB/decade.", "The lag-lead compensator has 2 poles and 2 zeros, so it adds no net slope at high frequency. $G(s)$ has 3 poles, so the slope is $-60$ dB/decade: **(c)**."),
"5.20": ("C", "Resonant frequency $\\omega_r=\\omega_n\\sqrt{1-2\\xi^2}$.", "Taking the given function as the system whose frequency response is plotted: $\\omega_n=10$ and $\\xi=0.5$.\n$\\omega_r=10\\sqrt{1-0.5}=7.07$ rad/s: **(c)**."),
"5.21": ("B", "Same plot as 5.16.", "The plot starts at $+1$ ($\\omega=0$) and tends to the origin along the negative real axis, i.e. DC gain 1 and phase $\\to-180^\\circ$. This is $\\dfrac1{(s+1)^2}$: **(b)**."),
"5.22": ("D", "Phase margin at a gain crossover at $\\omega=0$.", "$|G(j\\omega)|=\\dfrac1{1+\\omega^2}\\le1$, equal to 1 only at $\\omega=0$. The phase there is $0^\\circ$, so $PM=180^\\circ=\\pi$ rad: **(d)**."),
"5.23": ("B", "Same as 5.12.", "Bandwidth sets both noise filtering and how well the input is reproduced. Both statements are true but neither explains the other: **(b)**."),
"5.24": ("D", "Degree of stability.", "Gain and phase margins measure how far the system is from instability, i.e. its degree (relative) stability. The other options misstate the definitions, and Routh-Hurwitz gives only absolute stability: **(d)**."),
"5.25": ("D", "Bode plot of a first-order lag.", "$|X(j\\omega)|=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1+\\omega^2}}$: 0 dB for $\\omega<1$, falling at $-20$ dB/decade for $\\omega>1$: **(d)**."),
"5.26": ("C", "Sharp cut-off in the frequency response.", "A sharp cut-off means a high resonance peak (1) and a more oscillatory, less stable system (3). It does not imply a large bandwidth.\n\nOnly 1 and 3: **(c)**."),
"5.27": ("C", "Stability margins.", "A stable minimum-phase system has positive gain margin and positive phase margin: **(c)**."),
"5.28": ("D", "Gain at phase crossover.", "For stability the gain at the phase-crossover frequency must be below unity, i.e. below 0 dB: **(d)**."),
"5.29": ("C", "$GM=1/|G(j\\omega_{pc})|$.", "Doubling the gain doubles $|G(j\\omega_{pc})|$, so the gain margin is halved (it falls by 6 dB): **(c)**."),
"5.30": ("C", "Frequency-domain and time-domain correspondences.", "- Bandwidth $\\leftrightarrow$ speed of the time response (3).\n- Phase margin $\\leftrightarrow$ damping ratio (4).\n- Response (resonance) peak $\\leftrightarrow$ overshoot (1).\n- Gain margin $\\leftrightarrow$ stability (2).\n\nA–3, B–4, C–1, D–2: **(c)**."),
"5.31": ("C", "$M_r=\\dfrac{1}{2\\xi\\sqrt{1-\\xi^2}}$.", "$M_r=\\dfrac{1}{2(0.421)\\sqrt{1-0.177}}=\\dfrac1{0.842\\times0.907}=1.31\\approx1.30$: **(c)**."),
}
